# quarrel configuration. Copy to `.env` beside the executable. Never commit `.env`.
#
# Everything except live trading works with this file absent entirely: indexing,
# backtesting, the feed and TEST mode all run with no configuration and no key.
#
# Only the two settings below are read. If you find a third documented anywhere, it is
# stale — grep the source, which is the only thing that decides.

# --- network --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated, preferred first. Append #nologs to an endpoint that refuses
# eth_getLogs, so the request gate stops routing log queries to it.
#
# Leave empty for the built-in public pair:
#   robinhood-rpc.publicnode.com          state reads. Fast, tolerates ~8-way concurrency.
#                                         Refuses eth_getLogs.
#   rpc.mainnet.chain.robinhood.com       logs. Metered hard: measured 429s above roughly
#                                         two log calls a second.
#
# One private endpoint will beat that pair considerably. The gate raises its own
# concurrency to whatever an endpoint actually tolerates rather than to a number in a
# config file, so there is nothing to tune here beyond the URL.
#
# Example: RPC_URL=https://my-private-node.example/rpc
RPC_URL=

# --- the key --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only LIVE mode reads this, and only if no key was saved in Settings — the file written
# by the application wins when both are present.
#
# Prefer Settings. This exists for scripts and headless runs. Either way it is plaintext:
# read SECURITY.md before you put a key anywhere, and use a wallet holding only what you
# are prepared to lose.
PRIVATE_KEY=

# --- logging (optional) -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Standard `tracing` filter. Unset gives quarrel_app=info,quarrel_indexer=info.
# RUST_LOG=quarrel_live=debug,quarrel_app=info

# The data directory is a command-line argument, not an environment variable:
#   quarrel-desktop --data-dir /path/to/store
# Unset, it is your platform's application-data directory. See the README.
